      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town's name honors eight Jewish militiamen led by Joseph Trumpeldor who fell at the 1920 Battle of Tel Hai.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiryat_Shmona",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "First residents were fourteen Yemenite Jews who arrived July 18, 1949, followed by more Yemenis a month later.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiryat_Shmona",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1953 Kiryat Shmona was declared a development town, growing without a master plan as waves of immigrants arrived.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiryat_Shmona",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Kiryat Shmona sits just north of the Israel–Lebanon border, making it Israel's northernmost city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiryat_Shmona",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The city’s proximity to the border has made it a target of cross‑border rocket fire, notably during the 2006 Lebanon War when many residents fled to bomb shelters.",
